# The walled city's descriptions were written for this lesson.
lesson writing-setting-a-mood v=1 format=teach grade=5 unit=8 subject=writing levels=[easy, medium, hard] {
  title "Setting a mood"
  goal "Describe a place so the reader feels a mood, by choosing details, senses and verbs, without naming it."

  look {
    scene 36x13 {
      row all at=canvas(1, 1) space=2 align=end {
        passage p text="The library door creaked open on its own. Inside, the dust hung still, and the shelves stretched back into the dark." width=20 numbers=false
        library l columns=6 open=1
      }
    }
    say """
      The same library can feel calm or creepy. The writer decides which by choosing details: a
      door that creaks open on its own, dust that hangs still, shelves that stretch into the dark.
      Sounds, smells, light and weather all carry a mood, and so do verbs: a bell can chime or
      clang. The best descriptions never name the mood. They let the reader feel it.
      """
  }
  do {
    level easy {
      worked writing.mood-which k=0
    }
    show writing.mood-which k=2 easy-k=1 hard-k=3
    show writing.mood-sense k=1 easy-k=0 hard-k=3
    show writing.mood-detail k=1 easy-k=0 hard-k=2
    show writing.mood-sense k=2 easy-k=1 hard-k=4
    show writing.mood-detail k=2 easy-k=1 hard-k=3
  }
  story {
    show writing.mood-swap k=2 easy-k=0 hard-k=3
    show writing.mood-swap k=1 easy-k=1 hard-k=4
    level medium hard {
      show writing.mood-which k=3 hard-k=4
    }
  }
  try stars=2 {
    show writing.mood-compare k=0 easy-k=1 hard-k=2
  }
  try stars=3 {
    show writing.mood-write k=0 hard-k=1
  }
  remember {
    say "Choose the details, the senses and the verbs that make the reader feel the mood, and never name it."
  }

  grown-ups """
    The idea is that atmosphere is built, not stated: a writer chooses which details of a place to
    show, which senses to use, and which verbs to describe them, so that a reader feels a mood. The
    way in names the mood of a description of the walled city; the core finds the sense a detail
    uses, picks the words that do most to build the mood (past facts that would fit any mood), and
    chooses one verb that gives a mood asked for. The wrong idea to watch for is a description that
    lists facts (six columns, opened at eight) or names the mood (it was spooky). If the child is
    stuck, close eyes together and imagine standing in the place: what can be heard, smelt and
    felt. Off the page, read the first page of a ghost story or an adventure and underline the
    words that make the mood. This lesson takes the grade two description of a place (so it can be
    found) a step on, to a description for a feeling. England's programme of study for years 5 and
    6 asks for "describing settings, characters and atmosphere and integrating dialogue to convey
    character and advance the action".
    """
  level easy {
    grown-ups "At this level a mood is named once in the teacher's pen first, the moods are the plain ones (busy, creepy, peaceful), the senses are sight and hearing, the verbs to choose are for a fountain and a bell, and the two-star's change is the bunting the writer kept."
  }
  level hard {
    grown-ups "At this level the moods include tense and dull, the senses are touch and taste hidden in a verb, the verbs to choose make the market cheerful and the lanterns tense, and the three-star writes the market twice in two moods."
  }
}
